From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: irisrun <irisrun@localhost>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 12:43:00 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 12/38] Add TextField, SecureField and TextEditor for Linux

Without UIKit, text controls are drawn by SwiftUI itself: the text or its
placeholder, the input method's marked text underlined, and a caret where the
render core lays it out. Edits count UTF-16 code units, as input methods and
the Device Protocol do: committing text, setting the marked text, deleting
around the caret, and the keys Backspace, Delete, arrows, Home, End and
Return. Return in a single-line field ends editing and runs onSubmit; in a
TextEditor it starts a line. A tap focuses a field and puts the caret at the
nearest character boundary; a single-line field scrolls sideways to keep the
caret in view.

On Linux FocusState keeps its value in an observable reference, and
focused(_:equals:) hands text fields a target to keep in step with, since
upstream's focus system relies on UIKit and AppKit responders. onSubmit,
textFieldStyle (plain and rounded border), submitLabel,
textInputAutocapitalization, autocorrectionDisabled, keyboardType and
textContentType are there for forms to compile; the hints are ignored.
NavigationLink takes a title.

The stdout app takes the input as commands, with DeviceInput delivering it as
UIKit would: drag, fling, compose, type, delete and key besides tap. The host
also renders again for changes made while it renders, such as by onAppear.
